    "tag": "Health Writing",
    "title": "Beyond the Baby Blues",
    "desc": "A narrative health article opening on a husband's real fear for his wife, then walking readers through the signs, risk factors, and treatment path for postpartum depression.",
    "meta": "Article · Patient & family education",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Beyond the Baby Blues: What Every Family Needs to Know About Postpartum Depression",
      "“My wife changed after the birth of our son. She withdrew. She stopped seeing friends. She’s become a shadow of herself. Help — I’m losing her.”\nA Nigerian husband, desperate and confused, reaches out. He loves his wife. He wanted this baby. They planned for this baby.\nSo why does their home feel so heavy now?",
      "What He Doesn’t Know Yet\nWhat this man is describing has a name: Postpartum Depression (PPD).\nAnd it is far more common, and far more serious, than most people realize.\nPPD is not weakness. It is not ingratitude for a new baby. It is a complex medical condition involving emotional, physical, and hormonal changes that some women experience after childbirth. It can begin within days of delivery or emerge gradually over the first year.",
      "Recognizing the Signs\nPPD looks different in every woman, but common symptoms include:\n* Persistent sadness, hopelessness, or emotional numbness\n* Loss of interest in activities she once enjoyed\n* Difficulty bonding with the baby\n* Changes in appetite or sleep (beyond what’s expected with a newborn)\n* Extreme fatigue or loss of energy\n* Irritability, anxiety, or panic\n* Feeling like a failure as a mother or partner\n* In severe cases: thoughts of self-harm or harming the baby",
      "If any of these sound familiar, please keep reading. This is important.",
      "Who Is Most at Risk?\nPPD can affect any woman after childbirth, but certain factors increase vulnerability:\n* Personal or family history of depression or anxiety\n* Traumatic birth experience\n* Breastfeeding difficulties\n* Stressful life events during or after pregnancy\n* Social isolation or limited support system\n* History of abuse or trauma",
      "How to Help Her Heal\nFor the husband in our story — and for every partner, parent, or friend walking this road with someone — here’s what actually helps:\n* Seek professional support: A healthcare provider, therapist, or psychiatrist should be the first call. PPD is treatable.\n* Medication: Antidepressants and anti-anxiety medications can be safe and effective, especially when prescribed appropriately for breastfeeding mothers.\n* Therapy: Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) have strong evidence for PPD treatment.\n* Reassurance: Tell her you love her. Show her. Don’t stop. She may not believe it right now — but it still matters.\n* Support groups: Online or in-person communities for PPD can reduce isolation and provide shared understanding.\n* Rest and recovery: Help protect her sleep, encourage gentle movement, and take practical tasks off her plate where you can.",
      "How Long Does Recovery Take?\nEvery woman’s journey is different. But as a general guide:\n* Mild PPD: 1–3 months with treatment\n* Moderate PPD: 3–6 months\n* Severe PPD: 6–12 months or longer",
      "With consistent support, professional treatment, and love — full recovery is not just possible. It is the norm.",
      "So to the man who asked: your wife is not gone. She is fighting something she didn’t choose. And the most powerful thing you can do right now is stay — informed, present, and committed to her healing.",
      "She needs you to understand before she can begin to heal.",
      "Knowledge is the cure for the disease called ignorance. Share this. Someone needs it today."
    ]
